        "The Jade Emperor Pagoda, formally Phuoc Hai Tu, is one of the oldest and most distinctive temples in central Saigon. It was built in the early 20th century by a Cantonese immigrant to honour the Jade Emperor and other deities of Taoist folk belief. The architecture is strongly southern-Chinese, with yin-yang tiled roofs, brick walls and a front courtyard holding ponds of turtles and fish. Its most remarkable feature is the set of statues, made of carved wood and papier-mâché and painted in extraordinary detail, depicting gods, demons and scenes of reward and punishment in the underworld hall of the Ten Kings of Hell. Many locals come to pray for children at the shrine of the goddess Kim Hoa and the twelve midwives, and to ask for love and peace. Dim light filtered through incense smoke, candle flames and ancient statues creates a rare atmosphere of mystery and reverence. The temple has been visited by several foreign heads of state, adding to its fame. Located right in District 1, it is an easy stop for understanding the rich spiritual life of Saigon's people.",
